ustry practice and usually sufficient for these requirements without touching the core model architecture.\n    *   *Risk vs. Reward:* The proposal aims to make traceability a \"native architectural property.\" While intellectually stimulating (\"knowledge frontier\"), it risks introducing significant latency, making models harder to optimize, and slowing down the prototyping cycle (the user's main concern).\n\n    *   The goal is technical viability and speed of prototyping.\n    *   Integrating logging *into* the model architecture creates tight coupling between regulatory requirements and model design. This slows down experimentation. If the logging schema changes, you might have to re-deploy or even re-train/modify the architecture.\n    *   Standard middleware (post-hoc instrumentation) is more viable, faster to prototype, and generally satisfies \"automatic recording of events.\"\n    *   The proposal focuses on \"design-research\" rather than a pragmatic implementation path for compliance.\n\n    *   Rationale: Integrating logging directly into model architectures introduces unnecessary complexity and computational overhead that slows down prototyping and deployment cycles. Standard instrumentation layers are more viable and sufficient to meet the regulatory requirements of Article 12.\n    *   Decision: Reject.\n    *   Promise: 30 (It's an interesting academic exercise, but as a Dev Engineer focusing on viability/speed, it's a bad move for implementation).",
