        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2697\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Transforming a prescriptive regulatory mandate into a theoretical research program risks over-engineering and consumes scarce resources on academic rigor where simple engineering suffices. The marginal gain of 'minimal' logging does not justify the high cost of developing a novel mathematical framework for event selection when compliance is the primary objective.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 25\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Lab Director at an R&D institution.\nResource-allocation efficiency, balance between scientific ambition vs. funding constraints, effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\nArticle 12 (Record-keeping) and Recital 71 of the EU AI Act regarding high-risk AI systems.\n\n        *   High-risk AI must allow automatic logging over its lifetime.\n        *   Logs must enable traceability for risk identification, post-market monitoring, and operational monitoring.\n        *   Specific requirements for Annex III point 1(a) (start/end time, reference database, input data match, person verifying).\n\n        *   Independent screening based *only* on the Lab Director's vantage point.\n        *   Approve = commit scarce implementation resources. Reject = do not pursue.\n        *   No quota; judge each proposal on its own merits.\n        *   Strict format: 1. Rationale (1-2 sentences), 2. Decision (approve/reject), 3. Promise (0-100).\n\n    *   *ID:* P2697\n    *   *Text:* \"Minimal Sufficient Logging for High-Risk AI.\" A research program to find the minimum set of events needed for Article 12 using an information-theoretic optimization approach. It aims to create a general methodology (entropy, causal-inference) and validate against Annex III point 1(a). Positioned as \"auditability science.\"\n\n    *   *Scientific Ambition:* Very high. Framing compliance as an \"information-theoretic optimization problem\" is academically prestigious and potentially pioneering (\"frontier of auditability science\").\n    *   *Resource Efficiency:* Potentially low/risky. This transforms a regulatory *compliance* task (which usually requires straightforward engineering: \"log X, Y, and Z\") into a *research program*. Research programs are open-ended and consume significantly more resources than implementing known requirements.\n    *   *Funding Constraints:* High-risk AI systems need to be compliant now/soon. Spending funds on *discovering* what is minimal