istrative overhead and boilerplate coding for researchers. By streamlining compliance, it allows more time to be spent on high-impact research while ensuring results are traceable from the prototype stage.\n    *   Decision: Approve (because reducing \"toil\" helps scientific output).\n    *   Promise: Medium-High. It's a very practical win. While not \"groundbreaking science,\" it protects the researcher's time and ensures the data generated during experimentation is structured for future audit/analysis.\n\n    *   Is this too much of an engineering task? The prompt says I am a Researcher concerned with the knowledge frontier and intellectual merit.\n    *   If the lab spends resources on a \"scaffolding library,\" does that detract from research? No, it likely *enables* faster iteration.\n    *   Does Article 12 demand this? Yes, if they are building high-risk systems. If researchers ignore logging until the end (\"late-stage integration bugs\"), they risk losing critical data about how a prototype behaved during its most creative/experimental phase. Thus, having it from \"the first test run\" is scientifically beneficial for traceability of experimental results.\n\n    *   Rationale: Standardizing compliance boilerplate reduces operational toil and prevents late-stage technical debt, freeing up research capacity to focus on core scientific objectives and improving the reproducibility/traceability of prototype experiments.\n    *   Decision: approve\n    *   Promise: 85 (Strongly practical; high utility for maintaining research velocity).",
