his, am I spending implementation resources on a research paper? Yes.\n    *   Does a theoretical bound help me build the system *now*? Not really. It's an indirect benefit.\n    *   Wait—the prompt says \"technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\" If we don't know what is \"relevant,\" developers often over-engineer or under-engineer. This study *formalizes* the requirements. That improves quality.\n    *   But the phrasing \"theoretical and empirical study\" strongly suggests a research path, not an engineering one. The instruction says: \"approving... means committing... to pursue it, not funding to explore whether an idea might someday work.\" This proposal sounds exactly like \"exploring whether an idea might work\" (finding bounds).\n\n    *   Rationale: While theoretically interesting, this is a research study rather than a concrete implementation tool; it risks consuming engineering resources on theoretical formalization without providing an immediate, actionable mechanism for prototyping compliant logging.\n    *   Decision: Reject.\n    *   Promise: 30-40 (It has value, but not for a Dev Engineer focused on viability and speed).\n\n    *Self-Correction:* If I am in an *R&D institution*, the line between research and engineering is blurred. But as a *Development Engineer*, my specific domain of concern is \"technical viability\" and \"speed/quality.\" A study on information-theoretic bounds does not increase prototype speed; it potentially slows it down by adding a theoretical layer before implementation begins.\n\n    *Let's reconsider the \"maximal data collection\" point.* Defaulting to maximal logging (the \"log everything\" approach) is a common engineering failure that leads to technical debt and performance degradation. A principled way to avoid this is highly valuable for *viability*. However, the proposal is framed as a \"study.\"\n\n    *Final check on Article 12:* The regulation requires systems to \"technically allow fo