rhead (storage/compute) can derail an experiment or lead to unusable results if the logs weren't captured from the start.\n    *   *Alignment with Article 12:* Article 12 requires systems to *technically allow* for automatic recording over the lifetime of the system. Designing this into the project from \"day one\" is scientifically sound and prevents retrospective hacking of logging systems, which often leads to poor data quality.\n\n    *   The proposal ensures that regulatory requirements (which are essentially data collection requirements) are integrated into the research design rather than treated as an after-the-fact compliance checkbox. This protects the scientific integrity of the output by ensuring traceability is baked in.\n    *   While it adds a small amount of overhead at the start, it prevents massive technical debt and resource reallocation during the critical development phase.\n\n    *   Rationale: Integrating regulatory scoping into the initial research design ensures that traceability and data logging requirements are treated as fundamental technical specifications rather than afterthoughts, preserving scientific integrity and resource efficiency.\n    *   Decision: approve.\n    *   Promise: 85 (Highly useful for preventing \"compliance debt\" which disrupts research flow).",
