tributes to scientific knowledge.\n        *   Wait, the prompt says my domain of concern is \"knowledge frontier, intellectual merit... and broader scientific impact.\" This proposal is purely an operational/compliance mechanism. It does not propose new research, a new method of AI development, or a study on logging efficacy. It's an administrative gate.\n        *   Does it *hinder* research? Not really, though \"mandatory gates\" can sometimes slow down rapid experimentation in the early stages (\"development to staging\"). But the proposal claims engineers work within their current sprint cadence.\n        *   Actually, for high-risk AI systems, if we want the results of our R&D to have \"intellectual merit\" and be acceptable to regulators/the scientific community (especially regarding safety and transparency), having a rigorous, automated way to ensure traceability is actually a prerequisite for high-quality science. Without logs, you can't do post-hoc analysis of \"situations that may result in... risk.\"\n\n    *   Is this something a *Researcher* would back?\n        *   If I am focusing purely on the \"frontier\" and \"intellectual merit,\" this is boring. It's just plumbing.\n        *   But if I consider \"scientific impact,\" the ability to audit an AI system is what separates \"black box alchemy\" from \"science.\" Systematic logging enables empirical analysis of failure modes, which is core to scientific progress in AI safety and reliability.\n        *   The proposal ensures that compliance isn't a bolt-on at the end, meaning researchers don't have to scramble to reconstruct data they forgot to log—which often leads to scientific inaccuracies or inability to replicate findings.\n\n    *   *Rationale:* While primarily an engineering task, automating traceability via CI/CD gates is essential for ensuring that R&D outputs are empirically verifiable and reproducible, which is a prerequisite for intellectual merit in high-risk AI research. It prevents the