sable tooling: Creates templates/schemas for *all* subsequent projects, reducing redundant effort across different teams.\n    *   *Cons:*\n        *   Two-year duration is a significant time commitment before seeing \"tooling\" results.\n        *   It's research-heavy (\"develop formal methods\") rather than just implementing existing logging libraries.\n\n    *   Is this an efficient use of resources? Yes, because if the lab develops multiple high-risk systems, solving the logging problem once at a systemic level is more efficient than per-project ad-hoc implementation.\n    *   Does it balance ambition and funding? It's ambitious but grounded in a legal necessity (the EU AI Act).\n    *   Is it an \"implementation\" or just \"exploration\"? The proposal mentions producing \"directly applicable compliance tooling,\" which suggests a tangible deliverable rather than open-ended exploration.\n\n    *   Rationale: This approach creates scalable, reusable infrastructure that reduces the per-project overhead of Article 12 compliance while contributing to our scientific leadership in observability. By focusing on \"computationally cheap\" methods, it ensures that regulatory requirements do not compromise system performance or inflate operational costs.\n    *   Decision: approve.\n    *   Promise: 85 (High value due to the synergy between research and regulation, though two years is a substantial commitment).",
