e stale, which is a common failure point in compliance.\n    *   *Market Reputation:* Being known as an institution that treats compliance as \"living knowledge\" rather than a \"static artifact\" signals maturity and reliability to partners and regulators. It elevates the R&D process from \"hacking things together\" to \"industrial-grade AI engineering.\"\n\n    *   The proposal focuses on the *documentation/versioning* side of Article 12 and Recital 71. While Article 12(1) emphasizes *automatic logging*, Recital 71 explicitly states that \"this requires keeping records and the availability of technical documentation... The technical documentation should be kept up to date, appropriately throughout the lifetime.\"\n    *   The proposal's focus on linking artifacts (datasets $\\rightarrow$ models $\\rightarrow$ logs) directly solves the \"traceability\" requirement mentioned in Article 12(2).\n\n    *   Rationale: This proposal systematically addresses the EU AI Act's traceability and lifetime-maintenance mandates by integrating technical documentation with operational logs, mitigating the risk of non-compliance fines and reputational damage.\n    *   Decision: Approve.\n    *   Promise: High (around 90-95). It hits all the right buttons for a Compliance Officer: it's systemic, addresses specific regulatory articles (12 and 72), and transforms a liability into an institutional asset.",
