ity of prototyping.\" This proposal shifts focus toward sales, marketing, and customer-facing features. While it's a clever business strategy, from an *engineering implementation* perspective, it adds overhead (building the exposure layer) without improving the core technical quality or efficiency of the logging system itself.\n    *   *Regulatory Alignment:* Article 12 requires the *system* to technically allow for recording events. It does not require providing a dashboard to clients. Providing such a feature is an \"extra.\"\n\n    *   The proposal asks Engineering to build a specific exposure layer (endpoint/dashboard). This consumes resources that could be used for the actual technical implementation of Article 12's logging requirements or other R&D priorities.\n    *   It doesn't solve the *technical challenge* of logging; it solves a *sales challenge*.\n    *   As an engineer focused on viability and speed, I see this as \"feature creep\" that complicates the prototype/system without improving its fundamental technical adherence to the law (which is just about the logs existing).\n\n    *   Rationale: This proposal focuses on customer-facing exposure and marketing rather than the core technical implementation of Article 12, adding unnecessary engineering overhead for a dashboard/API. It introduces potential security risks by exposing internal logging architectures without advancing the underlying system's technical viability.\n    *   Decision: Reject. (Because it's not about *making* the system compliant; it's about *showing* it's compliant through extra features that slow down prototyping).\n    *   Promise: Low (maybe 20-30 because it is a valid business strategy, but from a Dev Engineer's perspective of resource allocation for technical viability/speed, it's a distraction).\n\n    Wait, let me re-read the prompt. \"Approve every proposal that clears your bar.\" Is my bar strictly \"does this help with technical viability/prototyping?\" Yes. Does building 