e lab is not just compliant but is shaping the standards of compliance. This enhances reputation as a \"responsible AI\" leader.\n\n    *   The proposal isn't about *implementing* the logs (engineering), but creating the *framework* for what should be logged (policy/methodology).\n    *   From a Compliance Officer's view: Having a documented, principled framework is a massive asset during an audit. Instead of saying \"we thought this was enough,\" we can say \"we followed our published, peer-reviewed methodology based on internal post-mortems and external consultation.\" This transforms compliance from reactive to proactive/defensible.\n    *   The cost is the time to write the paper and conduct interviews, but the benefit is a standardized approach across all high-risk systems in the lab, reducing fragmentation.\n\n    *   Rationale: Establishing a principled, documented framework for logging provides a defensible audit trail and reduces regulatory risk by moving beyond minimum requirements to a scientifically grounded standard. It simultaneously bolsters our reputation as an industry leader in AI governance.\n    *   Decision: Approve.\n    *   Promise: High (e.g., 85-90). While it's \"academic,\" the practical output is a specification that makes engineering compliance much easier and more robust.",
