ant reporting\" might not align with the *actual* pace of technical change in a fast-moving R&D environment.\n\n    *   As a Dev Engineer, my main concern is: \"Will this slow me down?\" and \"Is this sustainable?\".\n    *   Pros: No new administrative overhead/bureaucracy (no dedicated compliance staff to negotiate with). It uses people who actually understand the technical details (senior engineers/postdocs).\n    *   Cons: Documentation is often a chore that takes away from prototyping. Adding it to a postdoc's plate can lead to rushed, low-quality documentation right before a milestone, rather than living documentation. However, compared to a separate compliance team trying to \"extract\" info from the devs via interviews, this is usually more efficient.\n    *   Crucial Point: The prompt asks about *technical viability*. This proposal isn't about building a feature; it's about how the work of maintaining the technical documentation (required by Recital 71) is managed.\n\n    *   Is it viable? Yes, it's just a management shift.\n    *   Does it help speed/quality of prototyping? By avoiding \"compliance staff\" bureaucracy and keeping the knowledge with the builders, it likely preserves quality better than an external auditor would, although it adds to the builder's workload. It prevents \"administrative overhead from crowding out research headcount.\"\n\n    *   Rationale: Integrating documentation duties into existing technical roles and milestones minimizes administrative friction and ensures that those with the deepest system knowledge are responsible for the records. This avoids the inefficiency of a separate compliance layer trying to translate technical changes after the fact.\n    *   Decision: approve.\n    *   Promise: 85 (High, because it protects research headcount and keeps documentation close to the code, though there's always a risk of burnout for the designated custodians).\n\n    *   Rationale: Must be 1-2 sentences. Assessment from Dev Engineer perspective.\n    *   Decision: approve/reject.\n    *   Promise: 0-100.",
